 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: $O(1)$ to generate the static vertex buffer [G].
-- **Memory Complexity**: $O(1)$ for the static mesh data (36 vertices) [G].
-- **Performance**: Rendering requires one draw call per visible face if not instanced, or one instanced draw call per chunk/region if instancing is used [G].
-- **Strengths**: Simple to implement, minimal memory overhead for the mesh definition, supports instancing efficiently [G].
-- **Limitations**: High vertex count for large scenes if not culled, no topology optimization (no greedy meshing), potential for overdraw if backfaces are not culled [G].
-- **Culling Dependency**: The lack of face culling in the layout itself means the mesh generation step (handled by parent methods like [Face Culling and Visibility Optimization](3861-face-culling-and-visibility-optimization.md)) determines the actual vertex count sent to the GPU [G].
+-   **Time Complexity**: $O(1)$ for generating a single cube's vertex data. $O(N)$ for $N$ cubes if generating all vertices explicitly.
+-   **Memory Complexity**: $O(1)$ for the static vertex/UV/index arrays. $O(N)$ for $N$ cubes if not using instancing.
+-   **Measured Performance**: Not given in the sources.
+-   **Scaling**: Scales linearly with the number of cubes if instancing is used. Without instancing, memory and upload time scale linearly with the number of cubes.
+-   **Strengths**: Simple, static geometry allows for efficient instanced rendering. Easy to implement.
+-   **Limitations**: High vertex count if not using instancing or face culling. No automatic merging of adjacent faces.
+-   **When to Use**: When rendering large numbers of identical or similar blocky objects.
+-   **When Not to Use**: When high-resolution, smooth surfaces are required.
 
 ## Background
-The block face vertex layout is a standard technique in voxel rendering, particularly in games like Minecraft and its clones [G]. It provides a simple, unoptimized mesh for each voxel face [G].
+The block face vertex layout is a standard technique in voxel-based rendering, originating from early Minecraft-like games and voxel engines. It is widely used in real-time voxel renderers for its simplicity and efficiency [S1, S2, unofficial]. The specific index mapping and data structures described in [S1, unofficial] and [S2, unofficial] are common implementations found in open-source voxel renderer projects.
 
